WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.919 --> 00:00:23.559
Well, it's a great pleasure to be here, and
talk to you all. I have to say I've never

00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:26.319
seen such a big audience

00:00:26.319 --> 00:00:27.269
here. It's terrific. 

00:00:27.269 --> 00:00:31.949
So what I'm going to do is I'm going to
give essentially

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:38.949
the talk that I gave in Stockholm.
A few little add-ons I have to say, too.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:40.829
And I'll start

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:41.600
with a--

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.020
a little bit of a history

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:45.270
tour of how I

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:46.640
got where I am.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.390
Not a lot but some. 

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.520
And I should say that my

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.290
passion, and it really is my passion,

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:55.810
for trying to understand

00:00:55.810 --> 00:01:00.460
how macromolecules function in biology,
and understanding the biology of the

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.770
molecular level--

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.020
started from a lecture

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.000
that I heard from

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.640
by Max Perutz in

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:10.549
1963.

00:01:10.549 --> 00:01:13.030
It was a Dunham lecture at Harvard,

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:15.200
and--

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.800
and he talked about

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.760
his structural studies

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.010
on hemoglobin,

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.170
and 

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:22.049
Kendrew's

00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:23.299
studies on myoglobin.

00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:28.170
Now they had won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry
just the year before for their determining

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:28.850
structures,

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.960
and so Max

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.360
showed a stereo slide

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.880
of myoglobin, and the

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:35.830
auditorium

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:39.680
with at least as many people as here, or maybe
even more--

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.719
and it was a very large screen over his head
and

00:01:42.719 --> 00:01:46.410
we all had colored glasses on, and he
went back and forth--

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.859
you know how that works.

00:01:47.859 --> 00:01:50.590
And all of a sudden it popped into three dimensions.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:52.799
And the audience

00:01:52.799 --> 00:01:53.979
went, "Whoaaa."

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:57.540
I don't expect that will happen here.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.479
But none of us had ever seen

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:01.799
a molecule, a protein,

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:03.290
in three dimensions.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:04.409
And that was when I 

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:05.300
knew

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.909
that's what I wanted to do. I wanted to
understand

00:02:08.909 --> 00:02:12.989
biological molecules of the atomic level in
three dimensions, and that's what I've been

00:02:12.989 --> 00:02:13.699
pursuing.

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:16.989
So then I went and worked in Bill Epstein's lab. 

00:02:16.989 --> 00:02:21.040
at Harvard on the structure

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.209
of Carboxypeptidase A. Lipscomb won the prize in--

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:30.640
the Chemistry Prize in 1976 
for his work on boron hydrides-- not exactly

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.729
protein crystallography.

00:02:33.729 --> 00:02:36.769
And then I-- and there I got to interact with

00:02:36.769 --> 00:02:39.509
the Watson group,

00:02:39.509 --> 00:02:44.819
learning about ribosomes, which
they were studying at that time--

00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:48.349
and also Wally Gilbert on transcription
regulation.

00:02:48.349 --> 00:02:49.849
And then I went to Cambridge

00:02:49.849 --> 00:02:50.720
to work on 

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:51.849
chymotrypsin,

00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:54.049
and interacted 

00:02:54.049 --> 00:02:55.929
with 

00:02:55.929 --> 00:02:58.319
Francis Crick.

00:02:58.319 --> 00:02:59.239
And

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:02.400
it was really my interactions with those
individuals,

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.619
particularly at Cambridge,

00:03:04.619 --> 00:03:07.209
that triggered my interest in 

00:03:07.209 --> 00:03:08.499
trying to understand

00:03:08.499 --> 00:03:11.040
the simple dogma

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.609
of molecular biology.

00:03:14.609 --> 00:03:21.099
The central dogma, as you all know, is:

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:22.179
DNA

00:03:22.179 --> 00:03:27.299
is copied into DNA by DNA polymerases
and we enable studied many DNA 

00:03:27.299 --> 00:03:29.040
polymerases, and continue to do so--

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:34.360
and then the RNA is copied into RNA--
or, DNA is copied into RNA

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:34.830
using

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:38.679
RNA polymerase, and that's regulated, we've
been studying that.

00:03:38.679 --> 00:03:42.969
And then the tRNA has an amino
acid attached

00:03:42.969 --> 00:03:46.809
by amino acetyl tRNA synthetase,
and we've been studying that.

00:03:46.809 --> 00:03:49.640
So we went through this 

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:50.240
progression

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:51.559
and then about

00:03:51.559 --> 00:03:52.759
1995,

00:03:52.759 --> 00:03:55.899
we got to the point that--

00:03:55.899 --> 00:03:57.349
we'd reached

00:03:57.349 --> 00:04:01.789
the stage where we need to look at the ribosome.

00:04:01.789 --> 00:04:06.109
Now, when I was a graduate student at Harvard,

00:04:06.109 --> 00:04:09.559
this was a figure that Jim Watson made

00:04:09.559 --> 00:04:13.989
for a publication, and that was what was known
in 1964 about ribosome structure

00:04:13.989 --> 00:04:15.219
and function.

00:04:15.219 --> 00:04:18.299
It was known that there was an amino

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:20.859
acetyl tRNA-- didn't know what the structure was--

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:22.449
and there was decoding

00:04:22.449 --> 00:04:25.729
by the messenger RNA in the small subunit,

00:04:25.729 --> 00:04:28.909
and there was a peptidyl trine

00:04:28.909 --> 00:04:29.590
and the-- what

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.050
was called the P site.

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:32.970
But it wasn't known 

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.580
that there was an E site, the exit site;

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.900
it wasn't known that the polypeptide went
through a tunnel;

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.870
and there were no molecular

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:42.270
details

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:43.309
at all.

00:04:43.309 --> 00:04:44.460
It was known that there was

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:50.629
a translocation of-- a number of good things
known.

00:04:50.629 --> 00:04:54.819
Well, the ribosome

00:04:54.819 --> 00:05:00.020
is a very large RNA-containing machine. It's the
largest RNA-containing

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:01.870
machine

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:03.270
in cells. 

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:08.270
It has three thousand nucleotides in the bacterial

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.400
ribosome. Large subunit.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:15.340
34 proteins. And then the smaller
subunit that is about half that size. 

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.949
It's two thirds RNA by mass

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:22.120
and it's one quarter of the bacterial cell
mass. And that's useful--

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:26.010
if you want to do structural studies, you need to
be able to make materials, so that was very

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.219
helpful.

00:05:27.219 --> 00:05:30.150
Now Jim Lake started structural studies

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:33.210
in 1976, and this is using

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.060
electron microscopy,

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:35.940
negatively stained, and

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:39.289
he could get the shape of the ribosome,
and you see that's--

00:05:39.289 --> 00:05:41.120
it's pretty much right. 

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:46.080
This is called the crown view for obvious reasons,

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.319
and then this was the shape of a small subunit, and
then they sort of snuggle up to each other

00:05:50.319 --> 00:05:53.090
when they form the 70S. 

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:54.930
And then in

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.449
1997,

00:05:56.449 --> 00:05:58.080
Joachim Frank

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.029
used cryo-EM methods--

00:06:00.029 --> 00:06:03.240
single particle cryo-EM methods to get us

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:04.970
somewhat higher resolution

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:05.469
structure,

00:06:05.469 --> 00:06:08.149
something alike

00:06:08.149 --> 00:06:11.180
twenty four X resolution, and you see little indentations.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:16.469
He positioned the tRNA's-- the A site,
P site, and E site, and-- but

00:06:16.469 --> 00:06:18.770
not exactly accurately, and

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:19.930
guessed where the 

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:23.960
messenger RNA would go. 

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:25.729
So that's where it was

00:06:25.729 --> 00:06:27.459
in the mid-nineties, 

00:06:27.459 --> 00:06:30.199
and as I said, we had gotten to the
point where we 

00:06:30.199 --> 00:06:32.430
were thinking, what should we do next?

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:34.639
And Ada Yonath had,

00:06:34.639 --> 00:06:37.339
starting in 1980,

00:06:37.339 --> 00:06:40.199
grown crystals of the ribosome,

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:42.610
and then got crystals of the

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:47.229
species that I'm going to work on
the Haloarcula marismortui-- a large

00:06:47.229 --> 00:06:48.210
subunit.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:49.920
But then she published

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.680
a paper in 1995--

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:56.659
now, not everybody in this audience is going
to look at this and be able to decide whether

00:06:56.659 --> 00:06:58.909
it's good, bad, or indifferent--

00:06:58.909 --> 00:07:03.530
but this was a 7 x resolution electron
density map

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:04.520
that she

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:06.239
thought was

00:07:06.239 --> 00:07:10.770
moving along to understand the ribosome
structure. Well, many of us

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.730
looked at that and realized

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:15.439
that it wasn't actually

00:07:15.439 --> 00:07:17.429
correctly done yet.

00:07:17.429 --> 00:07:19.899
So we wanted to get into it. Well,

00:07:19.899 --> 00:07:22.989
the right person at the right time

00:07:22.989 --> 00:07:24.270
came. Nenad Ban

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:26.610
joined my lab in 1995

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:28.939
and he wanted to work

00:07:28.939 --> 00:07:33.729
on the ribosome. So I said, 

00:07:33.729 --> 00:07:36.179
"Great." And then I decided we should

00:07:36.179 --> 00:07:36.590
collaborate

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:40.139
with Peter Moore. I've known Peters since we were
graduate students--

00:07:40.139 --> 00:07:42.370
graduate students at Harvard--

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:45.749
and he's a long time ribosome expert

00:07:45.749 --> 00:07:49.709
and he likes to catch big fish, as you
can see.

00:07:49.709 --> 00:07:51.999
And of course, the ribosome is a big fish. 

00:07:51.999 --> 00:07:52.870
And so I thought,

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:57.060
yes, he is the right person.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.460
And then Poul Nissen

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:00.590
came from

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:01.359
Denmark

00:08:01.359 --> 00:08:03.290
a couple years after

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:04.399
Nenad Ban,

00:08:04.399 --> 00:08:07.189
and he joined in, and it really was Ban and Nissen

00:08:07.189 --> 00:08:09.229
who I think were the core

00:08:09.229 --> 00:08:10.479
of solving

00:08:10.479 --> 00:08:15.159
the structure.

00:08:15.159 --> 00:08:16.980
So then-- you might ask, well, what was the problem?

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.629
Why was this hard?

00:08:18.629 --> 00:08:22.210
Well the ribosome's big. But so?

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:26.619
And what does that mean?

00:08:26.619 --> 00:08:31.639
So we were able to make crystals following Ada
Yonath's procedure, but the crystals

00:08:31.639 --> 00:08:38.639
initially had a lot of problems. They
were very very thin, they were

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:40.630
not, well-- they often had

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:42.030
fragments...

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:43.390
They had problems.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:46.580
And then we had to improve that.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:51.470
But what you can do-- I'm not going to give
you an x-ray course here, you'll be be happy to know--

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:55.250
I give a semester course, or half-semester course,
if you wish--

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:58.490
but what I wanna say to you is, the problem is--

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:02.580
you can measure the intensities of the diffraction pattern,

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:04.360
but you need to get something else:

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:07.160
it's called the phase.

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:09.990
I won't go into what that is. But the way you
do that

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:10.879
is to use

00:09:10.879 --> 00:09:12.180
a method

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.510
that was--

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.720
initiated by Max Bruce-- one of the reasons
he got the Prize, and that is, you bind a heavy

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:19.389
atom

00:09:19.389 --> 00:09:20.720
to the crystal

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:22.670
and you collect the data again,

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:24.350
and you locate that heavy atom.

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:25.670
Never mind how you do that.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:28.010
And I say the problem with the ribosome

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:29.189
is like the problem

00:09:29.189 --> 00:09:33.190
of trying to measure the weight of the ship
captain

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:36.130
with and without the boat. Now if you have a sailboat

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:38.280
and a pretty good-sized ship captain,

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:42.030
you can measure these two and measure
this, and then subtract them, and you can

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:44.090
do it. And that's sort of like

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:46.620
a lysozyme-- 14,000 molecular weight,

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:50.290
and tungsten was 78 electrons. 

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:50.769
However,

00:09:50.769 --> 00:09:53.140
if you try to do that with the Queen Mary,

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:56.600
this is a bigger challenge.

00:09:56.600 --> 00:10:01.180
Because the error would be much larger and you
can't measure the weight of the ship captain.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:02.609
And I think this is like--

00:10:02.609 --> 00:10:07.170
the ribosome's sort of the Queen Mary of the
macromolecular assemblies. 

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:09.230
And so

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:11.720
this is not a heavy enough 

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:13.149
atom. So what one had to do

00:10:13.149 --> 00:10:14.030
was use

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:16.090
what are called cluster compounds.

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:19.820
So this one, tungsten 18, has 18
tungsten atoms,

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:24.740
and with the other atoms it has 2,000
electrons instead of 78. 

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:27.240
And the-- we use some other cluster compounds
as well.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.080
And it turns out that--

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:32.620
this is the most detailed crystallographic

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:34.390
part of the talk you're going to hear--

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:35.410
I won't go--

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:39.910
press on this for a long time--

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:43.580
but this is the important point and this is
what got us going

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.830
and the others as well, subsequently. So--

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:50.000
at very low resolution,

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.090
not that you

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:56.520
you all know what resolution is, but at low resolution
like 20 angstroms, which is down here somewhere,

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.990
the scatter 

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:02.150
from these cluster compounds

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:03.980
goes more or less through the ceiling.

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:05.250
And that's because

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:08.790
the scatter goes as the square of the
number of electrons, 

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:10.630
and so the signal is

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:12.709
about six hundred times larger

00:11:12.709 --> 00:11:15.180
then the signal from

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.340
just a single atom.

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:18.300
And here,

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:19.610
this is the scatter

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:23.290
with a hundred osmium hexamines.

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:25.690
So this is a very--what we did is we took

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:27.500
very low resolution data,

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:30.599
we got the signal, we measured the weight
of the ship captain,

00:11:30.599 --> 00:11:32.720
in spite of the fact it was the

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:34.020
Queen Mary,

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:36.050
located the heavy atom, and started

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:37.580
solving the structure.

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:40.160
And--

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:43.340
and this is where Joachim Frank was

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:46.220
using cryo-EM--

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:48.620
I would say this is like a Moore statue--

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:50.930
(not Peter Moore; Henry Moore)--

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:52.440
with the--

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:57.340
he's holding a torch and there's his head
there's his arm, he's kneeling down here--

00:11:57.340 --> 00:12:00.730
so it was very hard to make an atomic interpretation
of this...

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:05.010
So our first publication was in 1998,

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:08.260
in which we had a nine angstrom resolution
map--

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:09.640
very low resolution--

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:10.819
but we could see

00:12:10.819 --> 00:12:13.760
the twists and turns of the RNA, so we
knew

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:15.270
we were on the right path,

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:16.160
and besides, 

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:16.879
it looked--

00:12:16.879 --> 00:12:19.610
the shape was the same as the twenty angstrom

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:21.030
resolution

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:22.500
of reconstruction.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:23.689
And then the next year

00:12:23.689 --> 00:12:26.350
we got to

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:28.700
five angstrom resolution-- in fact

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:31.140
Ada Yonath and Venki Ramakrishnan

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:32.410
followed the same

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:33.980
route, and they got there, too, 

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:34.800
as well as 

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.780
Harry Noller,

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:38.470
and then that--

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:39.429
2000,

00:12:39.429 --> 00:12:41.970
we got to 2.4 angstrom resolution,

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:43.239
and you can't see--

00:12:43.239 --> 00:12:44.580
certainly not in this room--

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:49.660
the detail here, but we could actually position
every single atom. 

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:54.320
It took us several months to do that, but we
were able to do that.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:58.240
And so here is the structure that we
got, or a representation of the structure that

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:00.680
we got in

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:01.950
2000;

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:06.870
the RNA is shown with the red backbone--
sugar phosphate backbone-- and

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:08.060
white bases.

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:10.350
Very tightly packed, RNA, so 

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:13.029
we had no idea what RNA was going to look
like

00:13:13.029 --> 00:13:14.519
in this large assembly.

00:13:14.519 --> 00:13:15.770
Not a clue.

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:17.900
Proteins are in blue,

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:20.650
embedded around the surface.

00:13:20.650 --> 00:13:25.970
Again, the contrast isn't very large, but there is
where the substrate binds

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:28.460
and you'll notice there's not much blue there.

00:13:28.460 --> 00:13:30.100
I'll come back to that

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:44.600
momentarily.

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:50.430
So this is--

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:54.709
this is the large ribosomal unit
that was determined here at Yale. 

00:13:54.709 --> 00:13:58.180
This is a small subunit determined by Venki
Ramakrishnan

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:01.319
and put together using Harry Noller's
data

00:14:01.319 --> 00:14:03.460
on the-- 

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:04.890
on the 70S.

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:08.650
And here are three tRNAs borrowed as well.

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:11.170
And we're going to spend most of our time down
here

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:16.270
in what's called the peptidyl transferase
center, where peptide bond formation occurs.

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:18.910
That's where the amino acids are stuck together.

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:22.390
And then

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:25.209
this hole is where the messenger RNA goes in

00:14:25.209 --> 00:14:28.880
and Venki Ramakrishnan got a lot of
information as to how in the world

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:31.370
the decoding goes.

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:35.290
If I just rotate this around, you can see the very 
complicated

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:35.940
shape

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:37.820
that the RNA takes.

00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:39.669
It's pretty closely packed

00:14:39.669 --> 00:14:41.269
but there are little gaps.

00:14:41.269 --> 00:14:42.930
And the protein--

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:45.739
the RNA has little gaps; protein fits 

00:14:45.739 --> 00:14:46.980
into those--

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:50.720
into those gaps.

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:54.810
Again, you can see the complicated shapes

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:56.190
Now we split the

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:57.899
large subunit open like--

00:14:57.899 --> 00:14:59.040
like an apple

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:01.710
and this is a space-filling rendition. 

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:03.240
In white

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:04.540
is RNA--

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:05.850
you can see, again, 

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:10.440
that's pretty tightly packedÉ little-- little holes
here and there--

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:11.680
and the protein is

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:14.780
is penetrating into the RNA to sort of

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:17.620
act as a little bit of a glue to hold it together.

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:21.410
And here's, again, the peptidyl transferase
center, PT,

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:24.210
and this is a model built

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:27.000
where the polypeptide exit would be.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:28.730
That's about a hundred angstroms long.

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:32.420
So that's the exit tunnel. 

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:34.009
So there are a lot of proteins--

00:15:34.009 --> 00:15:35.950
I think there're about fourteen--

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:38.340
where there's a globular domain,

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:41.320
and then just a sort of wind-y,

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:43.740
highly charged piece

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:45.780
of protein,

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:46.660
and that

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:47.889
piece of protein--

00:15:47.889 --> 00:15:49.670
these pieces of protein sort of

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:51.779
fit into the RNA, 

00:15:51.779 --> 00:15:54.300
sort of hold it together.

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:58.670
And particularly I point out L2 and L3,
which have these big

00:15:58.670 --> 00:16:00.160
red parts,

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.570
and then if we look at where they are,

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:04.630
a close-up view,

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:08.770
here's where the active site is,

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:10.410
an analog--

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:12.660
substrate analog here.

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:15.060
There is the globular part

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:18.350
and the non-globular part just sort of threads in

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:19.720
around.

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:22.470
It comes close to the peptidyl transferase center

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:23.640
but it doesn't

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:27.040
actually make it all the way.

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:29.010
Now Francis Crick,

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:35.420
who always predicted good things-- I mean, right
things--he's amazing--

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:36.800
he thought in 19--

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:39.020
about 1968,

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:40.860
the following quote.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:43.480
I'm not sure if I can do it in a British accent.

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:46.150
"It is tempting to wonder

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:51.780
if the primitive ribosome could have been
made entirely of RNA."

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:55.280
And of course the reason for saying this

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:57.029
was that, how could you have

00:16:57.029 --> 00:16:59.930
a protein make the first protein? Because
how would you get the first protein

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:02.380
to make the first protein? It's a chicken and
egg problem.

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:07.440
So it was clearly a good idea and
really endorsed by

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:08.520
almost everybody

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:11.340
through the years, but there wasn't

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:16.130
any proof. So when we looked at what's the closest
polypeptide to the peptidyl transferase

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:17.160
center,

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:18.419
this is what we found

00:17:18.419 --> 00:17:19.650
in 2000.

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:22.449
Now, it turns out there's a little bit of a
disordered

00:17:22.449 --> 00:17:24.870
chain here that we did know about at that time,

00:17:24.870 --> 00:17:26.569
but it still can't get close enough

00:17:26.569 --> 00:17:30.100
to get to the peptidyl transferase center.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:33.320
So-- just so you don't miss the point--

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:36.190
the ribosome is a ribozyme. That means

00:17:36.190 --> 00:17:38.280
the catalysis is done 

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:40.450
by RNA.

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:41.770
Now of course, Sid Altman

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:43.310
shared the prize

00:17:43.310 --> 00:17:44.299
twenty years ago

00:17:44.299 --> 00:17:45.450
with Tom Cech

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:47.590
for also discovering the first

00:17:47.590 --> 00:17:51.430
ribozymes. 

00:17:51.430 --> 00:17:56.110
So, ok-- so RNA does the catalysis. How does it do it?

00:17:56.110 --> 00:18:00.620
What's the source of the ribosome's catalytic power?

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:04.320
'Cause RNA wouldn't necessarily be a chemist's
first choice as to what

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:06.780
he or she would use

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:08.030
to catalyze a reaction. 

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:09.120
So how does it do it?

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:11.860
Well this work was done

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:13.590
initially by Jeff Hansen and then

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:14.950
most of it

00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:17.390
by Martin Schmeing.

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:20.640
Jeff a postdoc, Martin a graduate student at that time.

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:21.690
And here's Martin

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:23.400
with his sailboat

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:24.930
on our salt marsh.

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:29.129
He had finished his degree but I wanted him to finish
two papers, so I said, "Ok, you can

00:18:29.129 --> 00:18:32.390
put your boat on our salt marsh and you just
hang out

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:33.690
for another six months

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:34.630
and--

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:40.540
and he did, and he published a paper in Nature on--

00:18:40.540 --> 00:18:43.010
I forget, cellular and molecular biology or something.

00:18:43.010 --> 00:18:45.670
So that was good.

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:50.429
I point out, by the way, that global warming
is a problem.

00:18:50.429 --> 00:18:54.080
This picture was taken five years ago 

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:54.809
and now

00:18:54.809 --> 00:18:57.730
the salt marsh has eroded, so this rock

00:18:57.730 --> 00:18:58.940
is up against water. 

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:00.810
And my geology colleagues

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:05.230
tell me that that's because the ocean
levels are rising and the salt marshes are eroding

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:06.550
very fast.

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:07.370
Well, anyhow--

00:19:07.370 --> 00:19:11.830
back to--

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:13.510
back to

00:19:13.510 --> 00:19:15.030
peptide bond formation.

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:20.650
So here is the reaction that's catalyzed; it's
kind of a dark slide,

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:22.460
I have to apologize.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:25.750
There's an A site tRNA that has an amino
acid on it, 

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:29.059
and it's the alpha amino group of that amino acid that

00:19:29.059 --> 00:19:29.720
attacks the

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:31.549
carbonyl carbon

00:19:31.549 --> 00:19:32.830
of that peptidyl

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:35.450
tRNA.

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:37.670
And that's in the P site. 

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:39.520
Well, we couldn't get

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:41.830
these tRNA's into our crystals

00:19:41.830 --> 00:19:45.570
so what we did is we used a fragment, and
biochemists have been

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:48.060
using a fragment reaction for many years.

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:50.710
And so one just uses CCA 

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:56.240
amino acid, or CCA and a peptide
analog.

00:19:56.240 --> 00:20:01.710
Then this goes on to form what's
called a tetrahedral carbon intermediate

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:03.080
with a negative

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:04.740
oxyanion here,

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:06.390
and then goes to product--

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:11.360
with the product winding up in the A site and has to
translocate back to the P site.

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:13.420
So what we did-- what we've done--

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:17.690
and I'm not going to show you but a very small
fraction of the work on this-- 

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:21.049
is solve many structures of intermediate
products

00:20:21.049 --> 00:20:23.879
and substrates to try and put it together
in a movie, and

00:20:23.879 --> 00:20:24.909
I will show you

00:20:24.909 --> 00:20:26.070
a movie

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:29.059
of this process that Martin Schmeing made, which is
always

00:20:29.059 --> 00:20:31.540
quite popular.

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:35.060
So again, if you take the 50S subunit and split
it

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:36.640
so that you can see the

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:37.760
exit tunnel,

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:43.320
here the three tRNA's borrowed from
Harry Noller,

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:46.010
the proteins penetrating into the interior,

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:48.280
and there's the peptidyl transferase center.

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:50.210
And we're going to be thinking inside this--

00:20:50.210 --> 00:20:53.559
this little box for a while

00:20:53.559 --> 00:20:55.860
and addressing the problem:

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:58.890
how does peptide bond formation happen?

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:01.890
Well, initially what was done

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:06.030
was to find

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:07.640
a CCA

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:09.299
to the

00:21:09.299 --> 00:21:11.350
A site

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:15.730
with an amino acid on it, and
it binds to what Harry Noller had described

00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:17.320
earlier as the A loop.

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:19.010
Hydrogen bonds to 

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:20.000
one of the base--

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:21.660
from one base pair.

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:24.070
And the CCA with a peptide

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:24.399
binds

00:21:24.399 --> 00:21:26.460
to what's called the P loop

00:21:26.460 --> 00:21:27.230
(Noller).

00:21:27.230 --> 00:21:28.860
Again, making

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:30.310
two hydrogen bonds

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:31.370
in this case.

00:21:31.370 --> 00:21:32.220
And there's

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:35.070
the attacking alpha amino group-- it has to attack-- 

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:36.600
we looked at-- we said, well,

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:40.210
that orientation doesn't look quite right, but, eh, 

00:21:40.210 --> 00:21:42.580
it's probably just not an accurate structure.

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:44.250
Well then, I went to--

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:46.700
Sweeden, to give--

00:21:46.700 --> 00:21:51.190
there're a lot of Nobel 

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:53.640
symposia that

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:55.870
one can get invited to sometimes.

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:57.430
I--

00:21:57.430 --> 00:22:00.890
I thought it was sort of like a qualifying exam.

00:22:00.890 --> 00:22:02.360
You're up there

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:06.159
giving you talk, and the Nobel committee
sitting in the front row, and you're like, 

00:22:06.159 --> 00:22:11.910
"Oh, my God."

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:15.200
The students understand that, right?

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:18.859
So the question was asked, why is
peptidyl tRNA not hydrolyzed 

00:22:18.859 --> 00:22:21.660
in the absence of A-site tRNA?

00:22:21.660 --> 00:22:22.820
If the structure

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:24.279
is all set up

00:22:24.279 --> 00:22:25.470
to go,

00:22:25.470 --> 00:22:27.840
well, why can't water attack

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:30.559
the carbonyl carbon instead of the amino group?

00:22:30.559 --> 00:22:31.830
And this was the same

00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:33.360
prob--

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:35.750
question that was asked by Kashlan [sic]

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:40.090
fifty years ago about hexokinase, and why
we studied hexokinase in the first place

00:22:40.090 --> 00:22:42.820
is because he asked, well, why isn't

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:46.200
ATP hydrolyzed in the presence of glucose? 
Because, after all, 

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:49.520
glucose is just a water molecule with a few
carbons attached.

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:51.919
And he said, Ah! there has to be a conformational change. 

00:22:51.919 --> 00:22:53.929
And that's--

00:22:53.929 --> 00:22:56.110
that's the answer here as well.

00:22:56.110 --> 00:22:59.270
And so while I was unable to answer the question
is Sweden,

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:03.360
Martin Schmeing was back at work in the lab and
came up with the answer

00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:06.660
by the time I got back.

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:07.860
And so

00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:10.230
what he found was that

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:13.960
if you don't have the A-site substrate bound,

00:23:13.960 --> 00:23:16.200
this peptide-linked

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:17.700
group, the peptidyl group,

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:18.750
is buried.

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:23.230
So this is where a water molecule would have
to be, either here or here, and it can't get there;

00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:25.920
it's sterically blocked by--

00:23:25.920 --> 00:23:29.630
sort ofÉ 

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:33.140
So what he did is he looked at several
substrates,

00:23:33.140 --> 00:23:37.450
and I'll show you a movie-- this will probably be
a little easier to understand--

00:23:37.450 --> 00:23:40.470
but the major point is that if he has

00:23:40.470 --> 00:23:44.470
CA in the A-site there's not much
of a conformational change

00:23:44.470 --> 00:23:47.110
but if he has CCA--

00:23:47.110 --> 00:23:50.630
and there are a lot of biochemical data for why one did this--

00:23:50.630 --> 00:23:52.789
what happens is this

00:23:52.789 --> 00:23:55.179
protecting base moves out of the way

00:23:55.179 --> 00:23:57.080
and there's a reorientation--

00:23:57.080 --> 00:23:58.860
and it's actually--

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:00.279
of the peptidyl group--

00:24:00.279 --> 00:24:01.909
and reorients so that it's

00:24:01.909 --> 00:24:03.029
now ready

00:24:03.029 --> 00:24:04.250
for chemistry.

00:24:04.250 --> 00:24:05.680
So what happens--

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:08.100
ok, here's the uninduced state. 

00:24:08.100 --> 00:24:10.150
Wrong orientation, really.

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:13.920
But then, when you get induced state,
this moves out of the way,

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:15.750
and you get this

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:17.710
in position to attack,

00:24:17.710 --> 00:24:21.320
which it can then do, and you get

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:24.150
this tetrahedral intermediate, and the oxyanion

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:25.000
that's formed, 

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:27.500
and that's what it would look like

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:32.100
with a substrate side-chain on. 

00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:33.360
So that's--

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:35.590
that's what it looks like.

00:24:35.590 --> 00:24:37.510
The ground state where it starts.

00:24:37.510 --> 00:24:40.480
And so there are two

00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:42.940
groups that could be

00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:45.990
involved in the chemistry of the catalysis--

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:48.200
now all catalysis by enzymes

00:24:48.200 --> 00:24:51.600
is activated by,

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:52.690
or enhanced

00:24:52.690 --> 00:24:57.580
by, the orientation of two substrates. It's entropy. 

00:24:57.580 --> 00:24:59.110
Jenks

00:24:59.110 --> 00:25:00.100
describe this

00:25:00.100 --> 00:25:01.650
nearly forty years ago.

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:04.950
So that's the biggest effect. All enzymes do
that. But what else do they do?

00:25:04.950 --> 00:25:07.549
Well, there's a hydroxyl group here from the

00:25:07.549 --> 00:25:09.110
P-site sugar,

00:25:09.110 --> 00:25:11.620
and there is a base here

00:25:11.620 --> 00:25:12.990
with a nitrogen.

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:14.870
Well, initially we thought, "Well,

00:25:14.870 --> 00:25:17.299
we wonder if that could be the base."

00:25:17.299 --> 00:25:22.880
Not our best hypothesis, I have to say, 'cause it turns
out to be wrong, as I'll mention.

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:25.109
And the other possibility is that

00:25:25.109 --> 00:25:28.850
this hydroxyl has an important role, and that

00:25:28.850 --> 00:25:32.110
turns out to be right.

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:34.130
So, Rachel Green

00:25:34.130 --> 00:25:36.620
mutated that

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:38.529
particular A to all the other--

00:25:38.529 --> 00:25:40.169
all other-- three other bases,

00:25:40.169 --> 00:25:41.010
and found

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:43.820
that with a full-length tRNA, it made no difference,
so

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:47.020
clearly that wasn't important for catalysis. 

00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:48.529
However, Scott Strobel,

00:25:48.529 --> 00:25:49.940
also working with Rachel Green,

00:25:49.940 --> 00:25:52.580
showed that if they removed that

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:54.090
hydroxyl group

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:57.010
from the P-site substrate,

00:25:57.010 --> 00:25:59.000
the rate went down

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:00.960
a very large 

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:05.060
amount. So clearly it was important for something.

00:26:05.060 --> 00:26:08.159
A similar experiment, but using fragments,

00:26:08.159 --> 00:26:09.070
had been done

00:26:09.070 --> 00:26:10.829
by Barta

00:26:10.829 --> 00:26:12.179
in Austria

00:26:12.179 --> 00:26:12.679
in

00:26:12.679 --> 00:26:14.480
2002,

00:26:14.480 --> 00:26:16.279
and she found

00:26:16.279 --> 00:26:19.630
just a couple hundredfold difference, but
she found, also,

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:21.940
that when this hydroxyl was removed,

00:26:21.940 --> 00:26:24.779
the rate went down, and so she hypothesized--

00:26:24.779 --> 00:26:27.840
using our structural information and her

00:26:27.840 --> 00:26:29.180
result-- she hypothesized

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:32.760
that this hydroxyl is acting as sort of a shuttle. 

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:34.000
So it's

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:38.080
acting as a general base, namely picking up
the proton from this attacking alpha

00:26:38.080 --> 00:26:39.400
amino group

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:42.040
and then acting as a general acid

00:26:42.040 --> 00:26:43.100
to provide it

00:26:43.100 --> 00:26:44.840
to the leaving

00:26:44.840 --> 00:26:48.260
three prime hydroxyl group.

00:26:48.260 --> 00:26:51.450
And there have been some experiments that have been done
subsequently, and that's

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:53.710
pretty well, I think, agreed upon

00:26:53.710 --> 00:26:54.830
as the mechanism

00:26:54.830 --> 00:26:58.740
and what the enzyme is doing.

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:04.770
Then another thing that enzymes do is stabilize
transition states, lower the activation barrier,

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:06.429
and is it doing that?

00:27:06.429 --> 00:27:07.860
Well, we used a substrate--

00:27:07.860 --> 00:27:10.690
again, synthesized 

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:11.919
in Scott Strobel's lab

00:27:11.919 --> 00:27:14.279
by Kevin Huang, 

00:27:14.279 --> 00:27:17.100
and this is an analog of the intermediate,

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:20.850
and the carbon is of mimicked by a phosphorus

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:24.340
of the oxygen by sulfur so we could
see it--

00:27:24.340 --> 00:27:27.000
and then there's a mimic of a side chain.

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:29.309
And this is quite high resolution,

00:27:29.309 --> 00:27:30.600
considering it's a ribosome,

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:31.870
I might say.

00:27:31.870 --> 00:27:34.910
So we could see where all the atoms are, and if we look,

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:37.950
we see that there's a water molecule interacting

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:38.649
with this

00:27:38.649 --> 00:27:40.320
oxyanion mimic

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:43.170
being held in place

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:45.150
by the RNA,

00:27:45.150 --> 00:27:46.240
or the ribosome. 

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:48.750
And so that

00:27:48.750 --> 00:27:50.729
could indeed be stabilizing, because

00:27:50.729 --> 00:27:53.450
there's quite a dipole moment

00:27:53.450 --> 00:27:54.900
on the RNA--

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:58.510
around the water, rather. Sorry. 

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:00.640
So, what's the source of catalytic power?

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:03.480
Substrate orientation by the 23S

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:06.080
RNA. Very important. 

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:08.970
A proton shuttle

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:10.520
by the two prime hydroxyl. 

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:12.270
And probably transition state

00:28:12.270 --> 00:28:15.740
stabilization. 

00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:21.870
Well, I think everybody's ready for a movie.

00:28:21.870 --> 00:28:26.040
And so this movie was made by Martin Schmeing.

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:27.600
Music was 

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:31.860
composed by others.

00:28:31.860 --> 00:28:34.110
And what he did is he took

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:37.980
each of the many structures that he

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:38.660
determined,

00:28:38.660 --> 00:28:42.980
and then morphed in between these structures, so
every time there's a little R-factor down

00:28:42.980 --> 00:28:44.000
this corner,

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:45.350
that's a real structure.

00:28:45.350 --> 00:28:48.230
and things in between might be

00:28:48.230 --> 00:28:55.230
a little Disney-esque. 

00:28:57.860 --> 00:28:58.759
Here's the

00:28:58.759 --> 00:29:01.840
large ribosomal subunit 

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:06.309
and there's the peptidyl transferase center,

00:29:06.309 --> 00:29:07.300
and so

00:29:07.300 --> 00:29:09.100
we're going to land

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:16.520
at the peptidyl transferase center...

00:29:16.520 --> 00:29:17.669
And we'll concentrate

00:29:17.669 --> 00:29:24.669
on the peptidyl transferase center.

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:29.210
We have now

00:29:29.210 --> 00:29:31.180
the P loop

00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:33.190
and the A loop.

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:35.660
We're just showing that.

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:37.320
Now we have the

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:39.250
peptidyl CCA 

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:43.790
P site substrates coming in, making its--

00:29:43.790 --> 00:29:45.250
two base pairs.

00:29:45.250 --> 00:29:46.970
And you see the carbonyl group

00:29:46.970 --> 00:29:48.840
is protected

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:52.490
by these two bases.

00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:59.490
Now we have the attacking A-site substrate

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:02.320
and--

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:04.120
making one hydrogen bond--

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:10.650
and this is the uninduced structure-- and then

00:30:10.650 --> 00:30:15.120
now there's a conformational change-- you get the induced structure, 
so this gets oriented properly. 

00:30:15.120 --> 00:30:19.760
There's a stabilizing water molecule. 

00:30:19.760 --> 00:30:20.730
Now we're ready

00:30:20.730 --> 00:30:22.340
for the attack

00:30:22.340 --> 00:30:23.700
of the

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:25.500
alpha amino group.

00:30:25.500 --> 00:30:31.720
The alpha amino group--

00:30:31.720 --> 00:30:35.900
ok, it seems to have worked. 

00:30:35.900 --> 00:30:39.090
So here's a tetrahedral carbon with the oxyanion

00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:39.380
interacting

00:30:39.380 --> 00:30:41.360
with a water molecule,

00:30:41.360 --> 00:30:48.000
and then it breaks down to get the product.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:50.470
And then the diacylated

00:30:50.470 --> 00:30:52.660
CCA--

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:55.460
or actually, tRNA in the real case--

00:30:55.460 --> 00:31:01.669
goes to the E-site, the exit site. 

00:31:01.669 --> 00:31:03.500
So now it's--

00:31:03.500 --> 00:31:08.960
this is made up, of course, we don't know how
it gets there exactly.

00:31:08.960 --> 00:31:09.370
It's

00:31:09.370 --> 00:31:12.580
going over two the E-site and the CCA will be

00:31:12.580 --> 00:31:18.400
interacting with the E-site. 

00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:24.790
The A76 is going to be stacked in
here

00:31:24.790 --> 00:31:28.420
and...

00:31:28.420 --> 00:31:32.990
Now there's no room for an amino acid, which
is why only the diacylated tRNA

00:31:32.990 --> 00:31:38.320
gets in here.

00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:42.320
And now-- now there's one more trip-- at the rotation of
180 degrees,

00:31:42.320 --> 00:31:43.860
the P-site

00:31:43.860 --> 00:31:44.860
peptidyl 

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:46.480
CCA goes--

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:50.480
or A-site, rather, goes to the P-site. 

00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:53.000
So that's...

00:31:53.000 --> 00:32:00.000
that's what we know about it.

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:08.600
SoÉ I gave a lecture once in Cambridge at the hundredth--
fiftieth anniversary of

00:32:08.600 --> 00:32:10.040
the structure of DNA, and

00:32:10.040 --> 00:32:13.869
I got to this point I asked Richard Henderson,
who's the head of the laboratory,

00:32:13.869 --> 00:32:16.490
"Should I continue?" And he said, "I don't think
you can pick top that."

00:32:16.490 --> 00:32:19.519
I'm going to continue because I want to move
on now

00:32:19.519 --> 00:32:21.269
and talk about the

00:32:21.269 --> 00:32:23.640
practical applications

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:24.559
of the ribosome

00:32:24.559 --> 00:32:26.280
structure.

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:28.600
And that is:

00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:30.230
it's a major target

00:32:30.230 --> 00:32:31.580
of antibiotics

00:32:31.580 --> 00:32:34.990
and we can understand how it works

00:32:34.990 --> 00:32:37.200
from our structural studies, and

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:39.850
the information can be used, and is being used,

00:32:39.850 --> 00:32:41.610
to design new antibiotics--

00:32:41.610 --> 00:32:42.899
not by us, but

00:32:42.899 --> 00:32:45.840
by Rib-X Pharmaceuticals, as I'll get
to.

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:49.150
So as you all know, 

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:53.640
resistant strains of bacteria are a major problem.
This was in the New York Times a couple years

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:55.710
ago

00:32:55.710 --> 00:32:56.289
about MRSA--
0:32:56.289,0:32:58.970	
methicillin-resistant staphylococcus aureus--

00:32:58.970 --> 00:32:59.440
causing--

00:32:59.440 --> 00:33:03.830
the number here is 19,000 deaths in
US hospitals alone.

00:33:03.830 --> 00:33:07.200
I've seen numbers up to 99,000

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:10.140
for all resistant strains

00:33:10.140 --> 00:33:10.669
everywhere.

00:33:10.669 --> 00:33:16.000
Anyhow, that's a large number of people who die from

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:20.340
lack of ability for the antibiotics to work.

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:22.190
And

00:33:22.190 --> 00:33:25.350
most of the antibiotics--

00:33:25.350 --> 00:33:27.710
sorry, about half the antibiotics target the

00:33:27.710 --> 00:33:30.460
large ribosomal subunit, the one we're studying

00:33:30.460 --> 00:33:32.300
And--

00:33:32.300 --> 00:33:35.910
actually about half of them do.

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:39.630
So it's a major, major target. 

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:41.179
Now this work was done by

00:33:41.179 --> 00:33:45.940
Jeff Hansen, who also did some of the substrate work. 

00:33:45.940 --> 00:33:47.899
This is one of our summer picnics. 

00:33:47.899 --> 00:33:51.480
One of the nice things about living in New Haven,
as many of you know,

00:33:51.480 --> 00:33:54.440
is the shoreline is gorgeous, and so it's

00:33:54.440 --> 00:33:55.950
really very nice. 

00:33:55.950 --> 00:33:58.850
So Jeff

00:33:58.850 --> 00:34:04.050
started by binding macrolides to

00:34:04.050 --> 00:34:06.260
the ribosome large subunit. 

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:08.050
And I'll--

00:34:08.050 --> 00:34:10.489
erythromycin is probably one that you know
about--

00:34:10.489 --> 00:34:13.169
We've taken you know what it is-- maybe all of you--

00:34:13.169 --> 00:34:15.239
a Z-pack, 

00:34:15.239 --> 00:34:16.849
Azithromycin--

00:34:16.849 --> 00:34:20.049
now it turns out that

00:34:20.049 --> 00:34:22.800
eubacteria-- and 

00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:24.939
Haloarcula marismortui is not a eubacteria,

00:34:24.939 --> 00:34:27.159
but an archaebacteria.

00:34:27.159 --> 00:34:29.919
Archaebacteria

00:34:29.919 --> 00:34:32.200
are really eukaryotes in disguise.

00:34:32.200 --> 00:34:33.700
They hung out with the 

00:34:33.700 --> 00:34:37.959
eukaryotes for about a billion years
after they split off from the eubacteria,

00:34:37.959 --> 00:34:40.069
and so they're very similar and--

00:34:40.069 --> 00:34:44.979
and so how can we bind antibiotics

00:34:44.979 --> 00:34:46.699
to this--

00:34:46.699 --> 00:34:50.809
eukaryote lookalike? Well it turns out you just go to 
very high concentrations. 

00:34:50.809 --> 00:34:54.019
And with some of them we've been able to do it.

00:34:54.019 --> 00:34:55.200
And so here

00:34:55.200 --> 00:34:57.509
that's where the macrolide ring binds.

00:34:57.509 --> 00:35:00.340
Peptide bond formation occurs up here.

00:35:00.340 --> 00:35:02.199
This is down in the tunnel

00:35:02.199 --> 00:35:03.539
and

00:35:03.539 --> 00:35:07.690
there's three of them here-- the rings
bind more or less the same way and they differ

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:10.099
by what the substitutions are. 

00:35:10.099 --> 00:35:10.910
That's how the

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:13.349
the different antibiotics differ from

00:35:13.349 --> 00:35:15.639
one from the other.

00:35:15.639 --> 00:35:17.390
So how do they function?

00:35:17.390 --> 00:35:20.799
Well, again, this is a split-open ribosome.

00:35:20.799 --> 00:35:23.239
That's the position of the macrolide.

00:35:23.239 --> 00:35:26.859
In green are residues of mutation--

00:35:26.859 --> 00:35:27.710
make the

00:35:27.710 --> 00:35:30.660
ribosome resistant

00:35:30.660 --> 00:35:32.079
to the antibiotic--

00:35:32.079 --> 00:35:34.429
and now if we look up the tunnel, 

00:35:34.429 --> 00:35:41.429
we can see-- there's where peptide bond formation occurs
and peptide comes out this tunnel, but-- 

00:35:42.679 --> 00:35:45.679
there's what happens

00:35:45.679 --> 00:35:48.939
there's where the antibiotic binds,

00:35:48.939 --> 00:35:51.399
blocking the exit of the polyeptide... 

00:35:51.399 --> 00:35:55.819
I call this molecular constipation. 

00:35:55.819 --> 00:35:58.659
Actually, I said this in Wweden and nobody laughed.
I think they

00:35:58.659 --> 00:35:59.859
all understand--

00:35:59.859 --> 00:36:01.010
their English 

00:36:01.010 --> 00:36:05.719
is really good-- but I don't think they understood
constipation.

00:36:05.719 --> 00:36:12.719
The word, that is.

00:36:13.339 --> 00:36:19.079
So we did some studies that try
and understand how these mutations actually

00:36:19.079 --> 00:36:23.279
effect the

00:36:23.279 --> 00:36:25.229
binding of the antibiotics.

00:36:25.229 --> 00:36:26.740
Now it turns out that

00:36:26.740 --> 00:36:28.839
in E. Coli, 

00:36:28.839 --> 00:36:30.529
mutation-- this A

00:36:30.529 --> 00:36:33.870
reduces to the binding constant of 

00:36:33.870 --> 00:36:36.089
erythromycin by 10,000 fold.

00:36:36.089 --> 00:36:38.679
And it turns out that in

00:36:38.679 --> 00:36:40.749
Haloarcula marismortui, 

00:36:40.749 --> 00:36:42.550
that A is a G. 

00:36:42.550 --> 00:36:44.679
Just as it is in eukaryotes. 

00:36:44.679 --> 00:36:48.009
And so it doesn't bind to a large

00:36:48.009 --> 00:36:48.699
fraction

00:36:48.699 --> 00:36:51.529
of antibiotics

00:36:51.529 --> 00:36:54.349
called MLSK,

00:36:54.349 --> 00:36:56.170
including erythromycin.

00:36:56.170 --> 00:36:57.210
Why is that?

00:36:57.210 --> 00:36:59.049
Well, when we look

00:36:59.049 --> 00:37:00.419
at where that

00:37:00.419 --> 00:37:01.470
nitrogen that

00:37:01.470 --> 00:37:03.700
on the G but not on the A

00:37:03.700 --> 00:37:04.140
lies--

00:37:04.140 --> 00:37:06.739
it's right under this hydrophobic ring, 

00:37:06.739 --> 00:37:09.539
it has to get these solvated, 

00:37:09.539 --> 00:37:10.490
and what's more,

00:37:10.490 --> 00:37:12.369
it doesn't fit as tightly.

00:37:12.369 --> 00:37:15.559
So that, we hypothesized, was the reason why

00:37:15.559 --> 00:37:16.749
this bound

00:37:16.749 --> 00:37:19.180
much less tightly. 

00:37:19.180 --> 00:37:23.920
So what was done by Daqi Tu, who's a joint graduate
student between Peter's lab

00:37:23.920 --> 00:37:24.850
and mine, 

00:37:24.850 --> 00:37:27.559
and Gregor Blaha, a post doc in my lab, 

00:37:27.559 --> 00:37:29.049
was to

00:37:29.049 --> 00:37:31.690
mutate the 

00:37:31.690 --> 00:37:33.160
Haloarcula marismortui--

00:37:33.160 --> 00:37:34.280
that G

00:37:34.280 --> 00:37:36.319
into an A. 

00:37:36.319 --> 00:37:39.560
Sort of the reverse of a resistance mutation. 

00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:44.529
A non-resistance mutation. 

00:37:44.529 --> 00:37:45.889
And when they did that,

00:37:45.889 --> 00:37:47.709
they found that all

00:37:47.709 --> 00:37:48.889
of this

00:37:48.889 --> 00:37:50.149
family of antibiotics

00:37:50.149 --> 00:37:51.719
that didn't bind previously 

00:37:51.719 --> 00:37:55.549
bound. Erithromycin, 

00:37:55.549 --> 00:37:57.039
Clindamycin,

00:37:57.039 --> 00:37:57.839
Virginiamycin,

00:37:57.839 --> 00:37:59.960
just a whole bunch of them bound very well,

00:37:59.960 --> 00:38:02.019
and if you look--

00:38:02.019 --> 00:38:03.729
with erythromycin,

00:38:03.729 --> 00:38:05.650
there's a G here

00:38:05.650 --> 00:38:08.989
even at three Millimolar, which is saturated

00:38:08.989 --> 00:38:10.059
concentration,

00:38:10.059 --> 00:38:11.739
it didn't bind.

00:38:11.739 --> 00:38:12.650
But

00:38:12.650 --> 00:38:14.959
with the mutation

00:38:14.959 --> 00:38:19.839
.003, which is about the lowest we could use in the crystal,

00:38:19.839 --> 00:38:20.619
completely

00:38:20.619 --> 00:38:21.439
saturated.

00:38:21.439 --> 00:38:23.049
So we estimated

00:38:23.049 --> 00:38:26.220
about a ten thousand fold in the binding counts. And then
if you look

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:30.200
at one compound, Azithromycin, that we could bind

00:38:30.200 --> 00:38:32.969
at very high concentrations, 10 Millimolar--

00:38:32.969 --> 00:38:35.200
both Haloarcula marismortui 

00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:40.319
and to the mutated

00:38:40.319 --> 00:38:41.369
ribosome--

00:38:41.369 --> 00:38:44.229
you see that it moves down.

00:38:44.229 --> 00:38:46.559
By about an angstrom. But not much.

00:38:46.559 --> 00:38:48.829
It's roughly in the same place

00:38:48.829 --> 00:38:54.029
And there is where that pesky little nitrogen
is that gets in the way of its binding.

00:38:54.029 --> 00:38:55.510
So that's why, I

00:38:55.510 --> 00:38:56.929
should say,

00:38:56.929 --> 00:39:00.460
use of these structures--

00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:02.599
well, it is useful to use these structures

00:39:02.599 --> 00:39:07.579
to design new antibiotics, in spite of
Haloarcula marismortui not being a eubacteria. 

00:39:07.579 --> 00:39:11.949
I might say it's more important to have
an accurate structure,

00:39:11.949 --> 00:39:13.260
perhaps, than a

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:14.249
eubacteria structure.

00:39:14.249 --> 00:39:15.799
I just want to point out that

00:39:15.799 --> 00:39:18.679
Ada Yonath's work

00:39:18.679 --> 00:39:20.799
early on--

00:39:20.799 --> 00:39:22.950
she had the antibiotic

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:24.690
erythromycin oriented this way, and 

00:39:24.690 --> 00:39:25.780
it's actually

00:39:25.780 --> 00:39:28.769
oriented this way.

00:39:28.769 --> 00:39:30.699
And we've now looked at

00:39:30.699 --> 00:39:37.660
another eubacteria and see exactly the same
thing, so this is the correct one.

00:39:37.660 --> 00:39:42.130
Now, different families antibiotics bind
in nearby places-- I'm not going to go through them all--

00:39:42.130 --> 00:39:44.729
here's a peptidyl transferase center.

00:39:44.729 --> 00:39:46.279
Peptide bond formation.

00:39:46.279 --> 00:39:49.149
Here's one that binds to what's called an A-site

00:39:49.149 --> 00:39:51.899
because that's where the A-site subject binds. 

00:39:51.899 --> 00:39:53.229
There are many that bind here.

00:39:53.229 --> 00:39:56.349
Here is Sparsomycin,

00:39:56.349 --> 00:39:57.709
Virginiamycin,

00:39:57.709 --> 00:39:59.499
Elasticidin,

00:39:59.499 --> 00:40:01.949
Chloramphenicol, which also binds up here,

00:40:01.949 --> 00:40:03.179
and

00:40:03.179 --> 00:40:04.329
the

00:40:04.329 --> 00:40:06.269
macrolide. 

00:40:06.269 --> 00:40:08.100
So what do you do with this information?

00:40:08.100 --> 00:40:10.130
And here is where I'm going to transition

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:11.319
into what

00:40:11.319 --> 00:40:13.389
is being done at 

00:40:13.389 --> 00:40:14.670
Rib-X.

00:40:14.670 --> 00:40:18.150
What you can do is you can take a piece of
one of these molecules

00:40:18.150 --> 00:40:19.370
and tie it

00:40:19.370 --> 00:40:22.289
chemically to piece of another one to make a hybrid

00:40:22.289 --> 00:40:22.750
molecule.

00:40:22.750 --> 00:40:25.239
You use that using the structure,

00:40:25.239 --> 00:40:25.969
computational

00:40:25.969 --> 00:40:29.630
methods

00:40:29.630 --> 00:40:34.289
developed initially in Bill Jorgensen's lab
in the Chemistry department--

00:40:34.289 --> 00:40:36.920
and these methods work very well.

00:40:36.920 --> 00:40:38.869
And so Rib-X,

00:40:38.869 --> 00:40:41.369
which is on George Street-- 300 George Street--

00:40:41.369 --> 00:40:45.579
has been working since 2001, when

00:40:45.579 --> 00:40:48.249
I and several of my colleagues-- Peter

00:40:48.249 --> 00:40:50.719
and Bill and others--

00:40:50.719 --> 00:40:52.379
founded it,

00:40:52.379 --> 00:40:55.329
and there they're getting good

00:40:55.329 --> 00:40:56.009
results.

00:40:56.009 --> 00:40:58.589
So here's an example.

00:40:58.589 --> 00:41:02.630
Never mind the chemical details here,
you can't see them anyhow and it doesn't really matter-

00:41:02.630 --> 00:41:04.479
so what they're doing is they're taking

00:41:04.479 --> 00:41:07.099
a one-compound linezolid

00:41:07.099 --> 00:41:08.649
which is 

00:41:08.649 --> 00:41:13.769
sold by Pfizer, it's a major new antibiotic-- new--

00:41:13.769 --> 00:41:14.960
some years old, but--

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:20.499
and combined it sparsomycin, which is 
basically a rat poison-- it's not discriminating. 

00:41:20.499 --> 00:41:22.400
So if you look at the selectivity,

00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:27.459
linezolid does select eubacteria but

00:41:27.459 --> 00:41:29.109
not eukaryotes,

00:41:29.109 --> 00:41:31.309
whereas sparsomycin isn'.t

00:41:31.309 --> 00:41:34.719
And then what is measured by the
microbiologist

00:41:34.719 --> 00:41:37.869
is something called the MIC. 

00:41:37.869 --> 00:41:38.919
It's not--

00:41:38.919 --> 00:41:42.559
M-I-C-K-E-Y is what many of us know,

00:41:42.559 --> 00:41:45.130
M-O-U-S-E, but--

00:41:45.130 --> 00:41:46.680
this is--

00:41:46.680 --> 00:41:51.299
M-I-C is the minimum inhibitory concentration.

00:41:51.299 --> 00:41:55.379
And you have to be below 4 to
be effective.

00:41:55.379 --> 00:42:00.679
And linezolid, as you can see, is quite
effective in many of these cases.

00:42:00.679 --> 00:42:05.209
But not all of them.

00:42:05.209 --> 00:42:09.690
And so these are the various combinations they made,
just tying them together, never mind the details--

00:42:09.690 --> 00:42:10.710
and they found,

00:42:10.710 --> 00:42:15.049
for example, one here that looks pretty good.
0.5, 0.5, 

00:42:15.049 --> 00:42:17.609
16 to high 128, still not good. 

00:42:17.609 --> 00:42:19.729
So then what you do is you do cycles

00:42:19.729 --> 00:42:22.499
of this. And again--

00:42:22.499 --> 00:42:24.160
a whole bunch of cycles,

00:42:24.160 --> 00:42:26.239
let's say 260ish, 

00:42:26.239 --> 00:42:29.079
and then you look at the MICs 

00:42:29.079 --> 00:42:33.139
against two tough strains.

00:42:33.139 --> 00:42:35.459
So, you see, this this was one that

00:42:35.459 --> 00:42:37.229
they didn't target;

00:42:37.229 --> 00:42:40.289
neither did linezolid.

00:42:40.289 --> 00:42:44.909
But there it is; it's down to two, and this is very good. 

00:42:44.909 --> 00:42:47.680
So they made compounds, now--

00:42:47.680 --> 00:42:48.749
they call it--

00:42:48.749 --> 00:42:52.919
they've got one that they call Radezolid

00:42:52.919 --> 00:42:57.909
and it will work against all kinds of resistance
bacterial strains.

00:42:57.909 --> 00:43:01.129
Here is Zithromax Z-pack, 

00:43:01.129 --> 00:43:02.859
doesn't work against any of these, 

00:43:02.859 --> 00:43:05.359
and here's a Vancomycin 

00:43:05.359 --> 00:43:07.139
and linezolid resistant--

00:43:07.139 --> 00:43:09.379
Vancomycin resistant

00:43:09.379 --> 00:43:10.639
strain--

00:43:10.639 --> 00:43:12.919
works very well against--

00:43:12.919 --> 00:43:15.489
these compounds from Rib-X work very well. 

00:43:15.489 --> 00:43:19.140
And it's not just MRSA that's a problem; there are

00:43:19.140 --> 00:43:21.529
other other kinds of--

00:43:21.529 --> 00:43:25.949
of resistant strains. 

00:43:25.949 --> 00:43:28.609
Again, enterococci--

00:43:28.609 --> 00:43:30.859
here's what Radezolid does,

00:43:30.859 --> 00:43:32.539
all fine;

00:43:32.539 --> 00:43:33.840
Vancomycin,

00:43:33.840 --> 00:43:34.659
terrible;

00:43:34.659 --> 00:43:35.729
linezolid,

00:43:35.729 --> 00:43:38.369
not good enough...

00:43:38.369 --> 00:43:41.960
So Radezolid's now made it through phase two clinical
trials,

00:43:41.960 --> 00:43:44.539
and so it's looking pretty good.

00:43:44.539 --> 00:43:47.059
Many of us are very enthusiastic about that.

00:43:47.059 --> 00:43:50.029
The company has several compounds

00:43:50.029 --> 00:43:52.589
that are in pre-clinical

00:43:52.589 --> 00:43:54.560
trials that were looking good,

00:43:54.560 --> 00:43:55.450
getting lined up

00:43:55.450 --> 00:43:57.229
to go into the clinic, including

00:43:57.229 --> 00:44:00.700
one that was a de novo design, just
starting from scratch,

00:44:00.700 --> 00:44:04.769
that works against gram negative bacteria,
which is also a major problem.

00:44:04.769 --> 00:44:08.289
So I think it's going to be very good. 

00:44:08.289 --> 00:44:10.089
And then I'm going to end

00:44:10.089 --> 00:44:13.329
the science part with just one last
thing:

00:44:13.329 --> 00:44:17.949
where do we go from here? I always get asked, "Well,
what are you going to do next?"

00:44:17.949 --> 00:44:19.410
And I always say, "The future is hard to predict." 

00:44:19.410 --> 00:44:21.640
But

00:44:21.640 --> 00:44:24.209
let me talk about tuberculosis.

00:44:24.209 --> 00:44:28.660
192 people will die from tuberculosis
during the next hour. It's a major,

00:44:28.660 --> 00:44:29.200
major

00:44:29.200 --> 00:44:33.189
problem, not in this part of the world, but 
certainly in the third world. 

00:44:33.189 --> 00:44:35.949
Although I think we

00:44:35.949 --> 00:44:37.459
could get into trouble as well.

00:44:37.459 --> 00:44:40.749
So what we have done

00:44:40.749 --> 00:44:42.839
is we've bound two

00:44:42.839 --> 00:44:46.569
compounds that are active against TB

00:44:46.569 --> 00:44:50.829
to 70S ribosome structure that we have done

00:44:50.829 --> 00:44:53.389
with three tRNA's bound. 

00:44:53.389 --> 00:44:54.679
And let me just say--

00:44:54.679 --> 00:44:57.769
and this is in press, it's

00:44:57.769 --> 00:44:59.259
not out yet--

00:44:59.259 --> 00:45:02.289
so it binds between the two subunits,

00:45:02.289 --> 00:45:07.169
and there's the tRNA-- that's in the
A-site-- now we're zooming in.

00:45:07.169 --> 00:45:08.959
So here's the tRNA

00:45:08.959 --> 00:45:10.140
that's being--

00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:12.579
it's interacting with the messenger RNA--

00:45:12.579 --> 00:45:15.910
this is what was found initially by Venki Ramakrishnan--

00:45:15.910 --> 00:45:18.490
and there are two bases that are flipped out--

00:45:18.490 --> 00:45:20.389
1492 and 1493--

00:45:20.389 --> 00:45:23.809
that are stabilizing this interaction.

00:45:23.809 --> 00:45:27.850
And what this compound Vyomicin is doing is
stabilizing

00:45:27.850 --> 00:45:30.239
this whole ensemble, just gluing it right there

00:45:30.239 --> 00:45:31.799
and stopping it. 

00:45:31.799 --> 00:45:33.549
Now why is this important?

00:45:33.549 --> 00:45:36.059
Well, it turns out that

00:45:36.059 --> 00:45:37.839
here's Viomycin--

00:45:37.839 --> 00:45:39.699
Capreomycin binds in the same place--

00:45:39.699 --> 00:45:41.549
and there are two compounds that

00:45:41.549 --> 00:45:42.699
Venki

00:45:42.699 --> 00:45:45.689
found were

00:45:45.689 --> 00:45:47.249
bound to the small subunit:

00:45:47.249 --> 00:45:48.550
Hygromycin B

00:45:48.550 --> 00:45:50.589
and Paromomycin.

00:45:50.589 --> 00:45:52.939
And, once again, one can only think:

00:45:52.939 --> 00:45:53.979
well, maybe

00:45:53.979 --> 00:46:00.209
the Rib-X trick will work here, and maybe
one can make new antibiotics.

00:46:00.209 --> 00:46:03.079
And there's a strain called XDR

00:46:03.079 --> 00:46:05.739
that is arising in south--

00:46:05.739 --> 00:46:06.799
southern Africa.

00:46:06.799 --> 00:46:09.149
A major resistance strain,

00:46:09.149 --> 00:46:11.229
because resistance can--

00:46:11.229 --> 00:46:14.199
all antibiotics, no matter what the target
molecule is,

00:46:14.199 --> 00:46:15.370
and the question is:

00:46:15.370 --> 00:46:16.559
can that be

00:46:16.559 --> 00:46:17.779
combated?

00:46:17.779 --> 00:46:19.590
The challenge, of course, is going to be

00:46:19.590 --> 00:46:21.559
to get funding for that because

00:46:21.559 --> 00:46:24.989
this cures poor people, but

00:46:24.989 --> 00:46:27.759
hopefully we can 

00:46:27.759 --> 00:46:31.629
do something about that. So I'm going to stop there. I'm going to 
make some thank you.

00:46:31.629 --> 00:46:32.500
If I--

00:46:32.500 --> 00:46:35.509
if I may.

00:46:35.509 --> 00:46:38.169
A few people I didn't mention

00:46:38.169 --> 00:46:40.819
in Peter Moore's group: Betty Freeborn

00:46:40.819 --> 00:46:45.690
was the technician who made the ribosome 
early on, and Larysa 

00:46:45.690 --> 00:46:46.480
Vasylenka 

00:46:46.480 --> 00:46:49.639
made them subsequently.

00:46:49.639 --> 00:46:52.359
Ah, let's see, who else didn't I mention...

00:46:52.359 --> 00:46:55.640
Dan Klein worked on many of the structural aspects

00:46:55.640 --> 00:46:57.799
in the later stages

00:46:57.799 --> 00:47:00.339
and I have to mention that Scott Strobel 

00:47:00.339 --> 00:47:01.909
had his

00:47:01.909 --> 00:47:04.569
lab synthesize some of the compounds.

00:47:04.569 --> 00:47:08.720
And I should also mention my fellow

00:47:08.720 --> 00:47:09.809
Chemistry winners,

00:47:09.809 --> 00:47:11.319
Ada Yonath 

00:47:11.319 --> 00:47:16.339
and Venki Ramakrishnan. And this is before he discovered
he needed to shave in order to get through

00:47:16.339 --> 00:47:20.079
security.

00:47:20.079 --> 00:47:24.270
He said he always was, you know, patted down and
had all kinds of trouble,

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:26.289
but as soon as he shaved,

00:47:26.289 --> 00:47:28.489
boom! Right through.

00:47:28.489 --> 00:47:32.339
So in case you think there's no profiling,
you're wrong.

00:47:32.339 --> 00:47:33.960
So this was at

00:47:33.960 --> 00:47:38.159
another one of those meetings in
Sweden

00:47:38.159 --> 00:47:40.510
a few years ago, very nice

00:47:40.510 --> 00:47:41.819
location.

00:47:41.819 --> 00:47:44.569
And then I should 

00:47:44.569 --> 00:47:47.009
mention that I was able to take

00:47:47.009 --> 00:47:49.119
fourteen guest plus

00:47:49.119 --> 00:47:50.799
Joan, of course,

00:47:50.799 --> 00:47:53.659
with me to Sweden, and 

00:47:53.659 --> 00:47:56.839
I had a number of family members.

00:47:56.839 --> 00:48:00.159
But I was also able to take a Yale--

00:48:00.159 --> 00:48:02.239
former 

00:48:02.239 --> 00:48:04.529
associated Yale people who our President wants.

00:48:04.529 --> 00:48:06.269
Peter, of course,

00:48:06.269 --> 00:48:10.229
a pivotally important person-- Peter Moore. 

00:48:10.229 --> 00:48:11.919
And then there's Nenad Ban 

00:48:11.919 --> 00:48:13.209
and Poul Nissen.

00:48:13.209 --> 00:48:15.539
Nenad's now the head of a

00:48:15.539 --> 00:48:18.709
very successful laboratory in Zurich,

00:48:18.709 --> 00:48:22.689
and Paul is doing very well

00:48:22.689 --> 00:48:24.870
as the head of a structural biology

00:48:24.870 --> 00:48:28.210
in Arhus. 

00:48:28.210 --> 00:48:30.609
And then this is Jeff

00:48:30.609 --> 00:48:33.329
Hansen

00:48:33.329 --> 00:48:34.939
and Martin Schmeing.

00:48:34.939 --> 00:48:36.159
And I also

00:48:36.159 --> 00:48:37.649
