00:48:37.649 --> 00:48:39.850
Peggy Eatherton to come along because she--

00:48:39.850 --> 00:48:40.860
she handles

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:44.319
so much of my workload, particularly the
paper

00:48:44.319 --> 00:48:45.080
workload, and 

00:48:45.080 --> 00:48:49.859
she stabilizes the whole lab crew, making
them work together, so that's very helpful.

00:48:49.859 --> 00:48:52.059
So we had a good time.

00:48:52.059 --> 00:48:53.630
And I also have to mention

00:48:53.630 --> 00:48:56.049
the environment in which all this happened.

00:48:56.049 --> 00:48:57.799
In 1995,

00:48:57.799 --> 00:49:00.019
here were the people who were in

00:49:00.019 --> 00:49:01.030
structural biology

00:49:01.030 --> 00:49:02.739
at Yale. 

00:49:02.739 --> 00:49:04.660
There was the the WERMS group,

00:49:04.660 --> 00:49:06.249
called WERMS because it's Wyckoff, 

00:49:06.249 --> 00:49:07.520
Engelman,

00:49:07.520 --> 00:49:08.589
Richards, 

00:49:08.589 --> 00:49:13.619
Moore, and Steitz. 

00:49:13.619 --> 00:49:14.699
And then

00:49:14.699 --> 00:49:16.240
in the mid- to late eighties,

00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:18.239
HHMI started supporting

00:49:18.239 --> 00:49:21.900
and by 1995, there was Jennifer
Doudna,

00:49:21.900 --> 00:49:23.069
Paul Sigler, 

00:49:23.069 --> 00:49:26.999
and Axel Brunger. 

00:49:26.999 --> 00:49:29.200
This was a great group

00:49:29.200 --> 00:49:31.869
of--

00:49:31.869 --> 00:49:33.529
seven out of the eight

00:49:33.529 --> 00:49:36.579
are or were in the National Academy of Science. 

00:49:36.579 --> 00:49:38.419
Very, very distinguished group, and

00:49:38.419 --> 00:49:40.429
I have to say,

00:49:40.429 --> 00:49:42.319
science doesn't happen in a vacuum.

00:49:42.319 --> 00:49:44.180
It's very important to have a

00:49:44.180 --> 00:49:45.550
good environment within

00:49:45.550 --> 00:49:46.669
which to work.

00:49:46.669 --> 00:49:48.650
And this was a great

00:49:48.650 --> 00:49:51.229
environment. 

00:49:51.229 --> 00:49:53.090
And this is the hooha

00:49:53.090 --> 00:49:54.090
that happened

00:49:54.090 --> 00:49:54.549
after

00:49:54.549 --> 00:49:55.800
the presentation--

00:49:55.800 --> 00:49:59.509
a wonderful banquet.

00:49:59.509 --> 00:50:01.899
And the servers, I don't know they did it-- there were

00:50:01.899 --> 00:50:04.119
fifteen hundred people there,

00:50:04.119 --> 00:50:06.299
gorgeous tables and

00:50:06.299 --> 00:50:07.660
music and

00:50:07.660 --> 00:50:09.909
and lots of singing and dancing

00:50:09.909 --> 00:50:11.070
and

00:50:11.070 --> 00:50:12.049
some of us--

00:50:12.049 --> 00:50:15.189
there's Venki, 

00:50:15.189 --> 00:50:16.540
there's Ada Yonath--

00:50:16.540 --> 00:50:17.520
little hard to see.

00:50:17.520 --> 00:50:19.209
The Crown Princess.

00:50:19.209 --> 00:50:23.960
I'm behind the candlestick here--

00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:26.489
sitting at the main table.

00:50:26.489 --> 00:50:27.619
And here's what was on the 

00:50:27.619 --> 00:50:30.229
front page of the Swedish newspaper--

00:50:30.229 --> 00:50:32.339
the Stockholm newspaper

00:50:32.339 --> 00:50:35.569
the day after the Awards ceremonies.

00:50:35.569 --> 00:50:37.420
I naturally thought-- 

00:50:37.420 --> 00:50:41.640
"Gosh, isn't the Crown Princess lucky
that Venki and I got her on the front

00:50:41.640 --> 00:50:42.269
page."

00:50:42.269 --> 00:50:47.749
And then--

00:50:47.749 --> 00:50:52.519
then somebody pointed out that maybe it
was the other way around.

00:50:52.519 --> 00:50:54.309
So, in the future--

00:50:54.309 --> 00:50:55.880
who knows when the future is--

00:50:55.880 --> 00:50:58.419
the Nobel Prize will be given out

00:50:58.419 --> 00:51:01.000
by the now-Crown Princess, who will become

00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:03.269
the Queen of Sweden. So I'll stop there. Thank you. 

